  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 PM CDT Wed Sep 23 2026\n\nUpper trough axis from Baja California up into the Four Corners \nregion is bringing moisture through NM, west TX, and much of CO this \nafternoon, with an upper ridge covering the Deep South. Another area \nof moisture is noted on water vapor imagery streaming into the \nPacific Northwest, which is associated with a low just off the Gulf \nof Alaska. Sfc low pressure has deepened in the lee of the Rockies \nin response to the western CONUS trough, while so far today we \nremain under the influence of high pressure just to our east. The \ndrier air and southeasterly winds have helped to scatter out this \nmorning's cloud cover, leaving us with pleasant early fall \nconditions with temperatures in the 70s. The deeper moisture still \nresides out in western KS where some showers have begun to move \nthrough ahead of the main system. CAMs largely show the showers \ncurrently around Hays and Russell dissipating before they would make \nit into our CWA, and forecast soundings support this idea as well. \nMid-level moisture looks to increase overnight, but soundings around \nCNK don't really show the low levels saturating until closer to \nsunrise. The upper perturbation rounding the ridge should help to \nincrease lift along with the moisture in the western half of the CWA \nThursday, leading to showers and a few storms focused in that part \nof the area most of the day with a lower chance of rain making it \nfurther east. Moisture looks shallower east until later in the \nafternoon into the evening, so if any isolated activity does make it \ninto eastern KS, it likely wouldn't be until then. Additional \nembedded shortwaves look to push the upper ridge further south of \nthe area Friday into Saturday and increase DCVA across the area, \nleading to greater chances for rain those two days. It is not \nexpected to be a widespread continuous rain all day; therefore it \ndoes not look like a washout. Elevated instability looks low \nthroughout the next few days as well, so while some rumbles of \nthunder are within the realm of possibility, severe weather looks \nunlikely. NBM probs of exceeding 1 inch of rain in 48 hours are in \nthe 60-80% range roughly northwest of an Abilene to Holton line, \nwith 30-50% probs south of that line. Given that rain totals are \nspread over a longer period of time, am not anticipating flooding \nbeing an issue.\n\nThe last shortwave looks to take a little longer to depart Saturday \ncompared to previous forecasts, probably in part due to a \nstrengthening low off the Atlantic coast slowing down our weather \npattern. As such, some rain may hang on in eastern KS into Saturday \nevening before dry conditions take hold for Sunday. While there is \nsome WAA with low-amplitude ridging to start out next week, the \ntrend is for a bit less of a warm-up with highs in the mid 70s north \nto low 80s south. This only looks slightly warmer than it would be \nlater this week with clouds and rain in play. Meanwhile, an \namplified trough looks to make its way across the western CONUS \nearly next week and bring another cold front across the area on \nTuesday, leading to our next best chance of rain Monday night \nthrough Tuesday.",
